sidgwick.md:5: argue/readings: S11 is read 2 ways: sidgwick.logic has "means_ought ∧ (means_ought → (established → ought_aim_general))"; sidgwick.logic.2 has "means_ought"; they differ when only "by "the general happiness is desirable" Mill means that it is what each individual ought to desire or aim at" and "the proposition that the general happiness is desirable (in the ought sense) is established by Mill's reasoning" are true, which the second allows
sidgwick.md:5: argue/readings: S12 is read 2 ways: sidgwick.logic has "∀x (desired(x) → reason_desirable_good(x))" and "established → desired(genhap)"; sidgwick.logic.2 has nothing from it; they differ when only "the proposition that the general happiness is desirable (in the ought sense) is established by Mill's reasoning", "people do actually desire genhap", "people do actually desire gh", "people do actually desire happiness", "a reason can be given why gh is desirable, in the sense of worth desiring", "a reason can be given why genhap is desirable, in the sense of worth desiring" and "a reason can be given why happiness is desirable, in the sense of worth desiring" are true, which the second allows
sidgwick.md:5: argue/readings: S13 is read 2 ways: sidgwick.logic has "¬∃x (person(x) ∧ desires_general(x))" and "desired(genhap) → ∃x (person(x) ∧ desires_general(x))"; sidgwick.logic.2 has "∀x (person(x) → ¬desires_general(x))"; they differ when only "people do actually desire genhap", "genhap is a person", "gh is a person" and "happiness is a person" are true, which the second allows
sidgwick.md:5: argue/readings: S14 is read 2 ways: sidgwick.logic has "¬established → gap" and "gap → only_benevolence_fills"; sidgwick.logic.2 has "∀x (person(x) → ¬desires_general(x)) → ¬established" and "¬established → gap" and "only_benevolence_fills"; they differ when only "the proposition that the general happiness is desirable (in the ought sense) is established by Mill's reasoning", "there is a gap in Mill's expressed argument", "the gap can only be filled by some such proposition as the intuition of Rational Benevolence", "genhap is a person", "gh is a person" and "happiness is a person" are true, which the first allows
sidgwick.md:5: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 2 ways: sidgwick.logic has "gap ∧ only_benevolence_fills"; sidgwick.logic.2 has "gap"; they differ when only "there is a gap in Mill's expressed argument" is true, which the second allows
