# question S11: Suppose "by "the general happiness is desirable" Mill means that it is what each individual ought to desire or aim at" and "the proposition that the general happiness is desirable (in the ought sense) is established by Mill's reasoning" are true and "each individual ought to desire, or aim at realising in action, the general happiness" is false. Is S11, "Now, as we have seen, it is as a “standard of right and wrong,” or “directive rule of conduct,” that the utilitarian principle is put forward by Mill: hence, in giving as a statement of this principle that “the general happiness is desirable,” he must be understood to mean (and his whole treatise shows that he does mean) that it is what each individual ought to desire, or at least--in the stricter sense of ‘ought’--to aim at realising in action.", true then?
# if yes: sidgwick.logic.2
# if no: sidgwick.logic
# question S12: Suppose "the proposition that the general happiness is desirable (in the ought sense) is established by Mill's reasoning", "people do actually desire genhap", "people do actually desire gh", "people do actually desire happiness", "a reason can be given why gh is desirable, in the sense of worth desiring", "a reason can be given why genhap is desirable, in the sense of worth desiring" and "a reason can be given why happiness is desirable, in the sense of worth desiring" are true. Is S12, "But this proposition is not established by Mill’s reasoning, even if we grant that what is actually desired may be legitimately inferred to be in this sense desirable.", true then?
# if yes: sidgwick.logic.2
# if no: sidgwick.logic
# question S13: Suppose "people do actually desire genhap", "genhap is a person", "gh is a person" and "happiness is a person" are true and "genhap has an actual desire for the general happiness", "gh has an actual desire for the general happiness" and "happiness has an actual desire for the general happiness" are false. Is S13, "For an aggregate of actual desires, each directed towards a different part of the general happiness, does not constitute an actual desire for the general happiness, existing in any individual; and Mill would certainly not contend that a desire which does not exist in any individual can possibly exist in an aggregate of individuals.", true then?
# if yes: sidgwick.logic.2
# if no: sidgwick.logic
# question S14: Suppose "the proposition that the general happiness is desirable (in the ought sense) is established by Mill's reasoning", "there is a gap in Mill's expressed argument", "the gap can only be filled by some such proposition as the intuition of Rational Benevolence", "genhap is a person", "gh is a person" and "happiness is a person" are true and "genhap has an actual desire for the general happiness", "gh has an actual desire for the general happiness" and "happiness has an actual desire for the general happiness" are false. Is S14, "There being therefore no actual desire--so far as this reasoning goes--for the general happiness, the proposition that the general happiness is desirable cannot be in this way established: so that there is a gap in the expressed argument, which can, I think, only be filled by some such proposition as that which I have above tried to exhibit as the intuition of Rational Benevolence.", true then?
# if yes: sidgwick.logic
# if no: sidgwick.logic.2
# question C: Suppose "there is a gap in Mill's expressed argument" is true and "the gap can only be filled by some such proposition as the intuition of Rational Benevolence" is false. Is the conclusion, "There being therefore no actual desire--so far as this reasoning goes--for the general happiness, the proposition that the general happiness is desirable cannot be in this way established: so that there is a gap in the expressed argument, which can, I think, only be filled by some such proposition as that which I have above tried to exhibit as the intuition of Rational Benevolence.", true then?
# if yes: sidgwick.logic.2
# if no: sidgwick.logic
